cmnf5wibm000auxs3bo1f6k85	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	Principles of Programming Languages\nComplete Answer Guide — 20 Important Questions\nQ1. Define syntax, semantics and pragmatics in programming languages.\nSyntax refers to the set of rules that define the correct structure or form of a program — essentially\nthe grammar of a programming language. It determines how symbols, keywords, and operators can\nbe combined to form valid statements. For example, in Python, if x > 0: is syntactically valid, while if\nx > 0 (missing colon) is not.\nSemantics refers to the meaning of syntactically correct programs — what a program actually does\nwhen executed. A program can be syntactically correct yet semantically wrong. For instance, writing\nx = 5 / 0 is syntactically valid in most languages but semantically erroneous because division by zero\nis undefined.\nPragmatics deals with the practical aspects of using a language — how programmers actually use\nlanguage features in real-world contexts. It covers coding conventions, readability, style guidelines,	[-0.010814003,0.0019296829,0.005141806,-0.06404359,0.006491138,0.019347357,-0.008799375,0.00055124017,0.014183599,0.0015152954,-0.004241271,-0.0115232915,-0.0211086,0.0059655886,0.11981331,0.012871453,-0.021217968,-0.009336708,0.016820936,-0.024775144,0.018997967,0.010481116,-0.01868601,-0.008733332,-0.0070572365,0.0048658065,0.011782783,0.0073826984,0.030345336,-0.007949055,-0.023341885,0.016798042,0.012066946,0.040963568,-0.017781321,0.017346743,-0.0077333157,0.00042605994,-0.0026402439,0.014187554,-0.021266542,0.0002956004,0.012973111,0.0029225945,0.0070863087,0.029123276,-0.0072009536,-0.018174477,-0.029629247,0.029325983,-0.0019469325,0.0041454267,-0.014248282,-0.19962628,0.00095985655,-0.011968879,-0.003353811,0.025352105,0.008669391,-0.0074073276,-0.01528211,0.0066936216,-0.021059478,0.0117168715,-0.0028026127,0.0015822466,0.039062947,0.0018059734,-0.010617502,-0.00621507,0.018982584,0.0064928983,0.0049377535,-0.003670