cmnf5wllv000huxs39poi152c	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	Semantics defines what a valid program means. Static semantics checks constraints at compile time\n(e.g., type checking — you cannot add an integer to a boolean array). Dynamic semantics defines\nthe runtime behavior. Example: int x = "hello"; is a static semantic error in C because a string cannot\nbe assigned to an integer variable.\nPragmatics — The Practical Use\nPragmatics covers how the language is actually used in practice, including idioms, style guides, and\nconventions. For example, in Python, using a list comprehension [x*2 for x in nums] is idiomatic and\npreferred over an explicit for-loop, even though both are semantically equivalent. Pragmatics guides\ndevelopers toward more maintainable, efficient code.\nQ6. What is polymorphism in programming languages? Explain with an example.\nPolymorphism (from Greek: 'many forms') is the ability of a single interface, function name, or\noperator to work with different types of data. It is a fundamental concept in object-oriented	[-0.0054131704,0.019360907,0.016114684,-0.063989684,-0.007221354,0.015932076,-0.003362307,0.024338314,0.01718373,-0.0072431057,-0.004413221,-0.011017556,-0.016721666,0.010331769,0.11941361,0.0118943965,-0.02578032,0.0071661724,0.010436811,-0.01015748,0.02283177,0.008673491,-0.01886508,-0.017412474,-0.016096244,-0.0027575942,0.0012136078,-0.0036270723,0.044677947,-0.0077921175,-0.024825502,0.029257124,0.0129830735,0.044225037,-0.012217255,0.010241322,0.003641862,-0.002393839,0.015134985,0.022639401,-0.017192587,0.003153961,0.0040706056,-0.002010181,-0.0035954101,0.010517785,0.0066215163,-0.019009,-0.02817559,0.024720673,-0.007180232,-0.005519118,-0.0054899743,-0.19300628,0.012820835,-0.0061938977,-0.014557638,0.030068958,-0.001988753,0.00050494296,-0.0047947858,0.008960976,-0.020464027,-0.000338361,0.014845157,-0.009414847,0.03674863,-0.005271945,-0.019153504,-0.0060885265,0.022173528,0.0012589616,-0.0006495353,-0.014045804,-0.009888704,