cmnf5wlz3000iuxs38ygda8h3	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	Polymorphism (from Greek: 'many forms') is the ability of a single interface, function name, or\noperator to work with different types of data. It is a fundamental concept in object-oriented\nprogramming that allows one entity to take on multiple forms, enabling flexibility and code reuse.\nTypes of Polymorphism\n1. Compile-time (Static) Polymorphism — achieved through function overloading and operator\noverloading. The decision of which function to call is made at compile time. Example in C++: defining\nadd(int, int) and add(float, float) — the compiler selects the right version based on argument types.\n2. Runtime (Dynamic) Polymorphism — achieved through method overriding and virtual functions.\nThe decision is made at runtime based on the actual object type. Example in Java:\nclass Animal { void sound() { print("Some sound"); } } class Dog extends Animal { void\nsound() { print("Bark"); } } Animal a = new Dog(); a.sound(); // Prints "Bark" —\nruntime polymorphism	[-0.0062890043,0.0067182267,0.023234144,-0.05889102,-0.028308429,0.02098385,0.0029930274,0.027308349,0.022532113,-0.011630936,-0.0019293865,-0.0036552323,-0.016935142,-0.0017571414,0.14005026,-0.0035709424,-0.009579261,-0.0035640397,0.014254687,-0.015861811,-0.00032928205,-0.018117828,-0.0040670144,-0.01142917,-0.0051840916,-0.004467181,0.007455902,0.0038189595,0.032034907,-0.0040047895,-0.0061406787,0.03270727,0.019175692,0.013440129,-0.009231599,0.005186078,0.006769955,-0.0065319943,0.008714796,0.020898217,-0.012532871,0.019620962,-0.0040718107,0.007940616,-0.00837801,0.0027504796,0.0026763252,-0.04704737,-0.022247693,0.021667583,-0.0015239832,-0.002469659,0.0063332943,-0.21203123,0.0035720912,0.0068064765,-0.0050709513,0.009335072,-0.0018602336,-0.0005318206,0.004237781,0.022741843,-0.0050020637,0.012783462,0.020134639,-0.016335875,0.032469828,0.001672206,-0.03349768,-0.00029120324,0.01366643,0.015346153,-0.013142412,-0.023912678,-0.0017499328,-0.02