cmnf5wolx000nuxs34sz93led	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	bound at runtime — x = 5 binds integer to x, but later x = "hello" rebinds it to string. Early binding\n(compile time) is more efficient; late binding (runtime) is more flexible.\nQ9. Explain how type equivalence helps in detecting type errors in programs.\nType equivalence defines the rules that determine when two type expressions are considered the\nsame (or compatible) type. This is crucial because the type system uses these rules to check\nwhether operations and assignments are valid, thereby detecting type errors before or during\nexecution.\nTwo Main Approaches\n1. Name Equivalence: Two types are equivalent only if they have the same name (i.e., they are\ndeclared with the same type identifier). Even if two struct types have identical fields, they are\ndifferent types if declared separately. This approach is stricter and catches more potential errors.\nUsed in Ada and Java.\n2. Structural Equivalence: Two types are equivalent if they have the same structure — same	[-0.002785083,0.004819746,-0.005088945,-0.055519383,-0.021959059,0.026207294,0.0035440645,0.0057130223,-0.00015302979,0.0053924825,-0.006836901,0.0013697706,-0.008926733,-0.00055381877,0.13357267,0.009159407,-0.014727209,-0.013272995,0.016660342,-0.016983187,0.010058303,0.013838147,-0.008163885,-0.010448211,-0.01400727,-0.004919598,0.018786922,-0.0047709574,0.03760472,-0.0062492634,-0.030387666,0.021577433,0.028447034,0.043174602,-0.0044695092,0.0095836185,-0.0064474246,-0.007925642,0.012945047,0.0151999565,-0.00613391,0.0005548887,0.010787066,0.020434298,-0.009934146,0.033912584,-0.006681579,-0.011893317,-0.037101977,0.029915843,0.0015146633,0.012602047,-0.0022719586,-0.1964066,0.009309236,-0.02184785,0.002390422,0.007686289,0.002173493,0.0042524813,-0.008449976,-0.00024419138,-0.01695637,0.010924432,0.026915714,-0.0078014093,0.029633258,0.0002452901,-0.012204071,-0.00951873,0.01713837,0.0026483142,-0.00013647281,-0.01667015,0.0073912763,-0.0164087