cmnf5wqqh000quxs30rbycaow	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	exposing only the essential operations (interface) that users need to interact with it. It allows a\nprogrammer to use a data type without needing to know how it is internally represented or how its\noperations are implemented.\nData abstraction is typically implemented through Abstract Data Types (ADTs) and classes in\nobject-oriented languages. For example, a Stack ADT exposes operations like push(), pop(), and\nisEmpty(), but hides whether it's implemented using an array or a linked list.\nAdvantages\n1. Simplicity: Users interact with a clean, simple interface without worrying about complexity\nunderneath. Using a Queue class just requires calling enqueue() and dequeue().\n2. Maintainability: The implementation can be changed (e.g., switching from array to linked list)\nwithout affecting the code that uses it, as long as the interface remains the same.\n3. Reusability: Well-defined abstractions can be reused in different programs. Java's ArrayList or	[-0.0072729713,-0.014068507,0.02523647,-0.05773881,-0.030849874,-0.0012734027,0.0050545144,0.016777309,0.0077047665,0.008709784,-0.018645106,-0.002830192,-0.005820257,-0.009974665,0.1287749,0.0019230259,0.006820831,-0.00444753,0.012803975,-0.023894629,0.01264927,0.009418685,-0.0041872864,-0.031067356,-0.0019574717,-0.009460643,-0.0063163647,0.0017842725,0.036566075,-0.002661766,-0.006977558,0.03057847,0.023549093,0.017948773,-0.0100315,-0.0011127101,0.008996967,0.009011918,0.008952193,0.01850064,0.010981028,0.02327228,0.011258938,-0.0054850555,0.0029015196,0.018100077,0.0058620702,-0.032146163,-0.028420923,0.0002381546,0.019693565,-0.008340403,0.0012734451,-0.21603975,0.023619493,0.005879682,-0.0077391714,-0.0006061962,0.008543833,0.006295745,0.006414777,0.011015423,-0.022667792,0.019036226,0.010476351,-0.03172465,0.019012349,-0.011334812,-0.03178475,0.010580704,0.01248999,-0.0028764722,-0.007290408,-0.0090696635,0.006976005,-0.004613322,0.018594773,-0.018876957,0.