cmnf5wt06000uuxs31jkqcovp	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	A class is a user-defined blueprint or template that defines the attributes (data members) and\nbehaviors (methods) that objects of that class will have. A class is like a cookie cutter — it defines\nthe shape, but is not itself a cookie.\nObject\nAn object is an instance of a class — a concrete realization of the blueprint with actual data values.\nMany objects can be created from a single class, each with its own state.\nExample in Java:\nclass Car { String brand; // attribute int speed; void accelerate() { // method speed\n+= 10; System.out.println(brand + " now going " + speed + " km/h"); } } // Creating\nobjects Car car1 = new Car(); car1.brand = "Toyota"; car1.speed = 60;\ncar1.accelerate(); // Output: Toyota now going 70 km/h\nHere, Car is the class (blueprint) and car1 is an object (instance). The class defines what data\n(brand, speed) and behavior (accelerate()) all cars share. Each object maintains its own copy of the\ndata.\nQ13. What are primitive data types? Explain with examples.	[-0.011295364,0.0045513823,0.019349035,-0.048920296,-0.011815823,-0.0017985112,0.037562978,0.006541393,0.013141777,0.0050684297,-0.005666611,-0.009642878,-0.02219919,0.0010828944,0.1399397,-0.015604492,-0.012426176,-0.025509512,0.021811098,-0.008497885,0.009463237,-0.001318625,0.0015104966,-0.013981809,0.004821021,-0.0029706706,-0.00989209,0.021507297,0.037889075,0.0067879814,-0.016423319,0.03277193,0.013964587,0.024546724,-0.012541591,0.006753461,-0.015095721,-0.011684256,-0.008591573,0.0016769605,-0.0014085649,0.003960272,-0.0013405668,0.010408639,0.012152099,0.013698011,0.0070860717,0.002570986,-0.019138485,0.0076723797,0.020172803,0.005966491,-0.011524748,-0.21360308,0.023429386,0.0014038789,-0.012495118,0.005102919,-0.008402626,0.0011648835,0.0043947753,0.0104242945,0.0059762727,0.013462949,-0.00016047254,-0.008146644,0.028791381,-0.0036598956,-0.012978599,0.009843864,0.015486012,0.0020525868,-0.009245824,0.0010206618,0