cmnf5wtdt000vuxs390hih5fp	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	(brand, speed) and behavior (accelerate()) all cars share. Each object maintains its own copy of the\ndata.\nQ13. What are primitive data types? Explain with examples.\nPrimitive data types (also called built-in or elementary data types) are the most basic data types\nprovided directly by the programming language. They are not composed of other types and are\ntypically mapped directly to hardware-level operations. They serve as the building blocks from which\nall other (user-defined) types are constructed.\nCommon Primitive Data Types\nInteger: Represents whole numbers without a fractional component. Example: int age = 25; in\nC/Java. Different sizes exist: short, int, long.\nFloating Point: Represents real numbers with fractional parts. Based on IEEE 754 standard.\nExample: float pi = 3.14f; or double e = 2.71828;.\nBoolean: Represents logical values — true or false. Example: boolean isLoggedIn = true; in Java.	[-0.008101954,0.0022468257,0.01975021,-0.052207716,-0.021631276,0.0141891595,0.026324159,0.00031405917,0.022091413,0.014477103,-0.0003583777,-0.0063420474,-0.011428147,-0.0046621505,0.13568419,-0.003969297,-0.0046197334,-0.01822023,0.020279255,-0.011639754,0.017405707,0.0011753185,-0.0012612565,-0.010148223,0.0027367012,-0.0113437725,-0.011112627,0.010076786,0.043660678,0.006365046,-0.017291281,0.027246328,0.012302217,0.02158686,-0.015061752,0.004335089,-0.012606415,-0.008986977,0.0023325502,0.0046987645,-0.0029062973,-0.006814345,-0.0006017252,-0.00080157386,-0.0015862981,0.014235038,0.016630799,0.009087724,-0.027644034,0.01436653,0.024388129,-0.002501427,-0.0013534644,-0.21433982,0.02688636,0.0035235542,-0.002372746,0.010427472,-0.005916688,-0.0050414605,0.014480819,0.0017952275,0.0027972846,0.009976313,0.008440567,-0.0046278313,0.030878969,0.0029293008,-0.018660322,0.012871601,0.019138072,-0.006588783,-0.004441806,-0.010331435,0.013060465,-0.015315775,0.02105768,-0.01882092,-0.011089241,0.02648407,-0.022