cmnf5wui9000yuxs36fl1hhet	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	Structured types enable the modeling of real-world entities and are essential for writing organized,\nmaintainable programs.\nQ15. What is type parameterization in programming languages?\nType parameterization (also known as generics or parametric polymorphism) is the ability to define\ndata types, classes, or functions with type parameters — placeholders for actual types that are\nspecified later when the type or function is used. This allows writing code that is type-safe yet works\nfor multiple data types without duplication.\nWithout type parameterization, a developer would need to write separate Stack implementations for\nint, float, String, etc. With generics, a single Stack<T> works for any type T.\nExample in Java:\nclass Stack { // T is a type parameter private ArrayList elements = new ArrayList<>();\npublic void push(T item) { elements.add(item); } public T pop() { return\nelements.remove(elements.size()-1); } } // Usage Stack intStack = new Stack<>(); Stack\nstrStack = new Stack<>();	[-0.014940667,0.016145315,0.009068115,-0.0581511,-0.01590495,0.009360242,0.024931831,0.01658828,0.019317856,0.011827344,-0.003137812,0.0071570575,-0.011915797,0.0039093797,0.12509969,-0.0064565497,-0.003614674,-0.0017969704,0.027793063,-0.019093731,0.012664575,0.008705703,-0.014422446,-0.018554864,0.00046270312,-0.010737166,0.010173698,0.0028876462,0.04182791,0.00055613735,-0.011839177,0.02820451,0.025348471,0.022660019,-0.012016973,0.0018610825,0.0026502898,-0.013759401,-0.0009961616,0.013608231,-0.012411746,-0.018272221,0.012730518,0.010639647,-0.007503329,0.016558506,0.0012882744,-0.009609652,-0.04168747,0.0146361645,0.0179635,-0.0002943305,-0.0042486116,-0.19612938,0.024071937,0.010180968,0.001159987,0.011216847,0.0035187488,0.010870171,0.011533661,-0.0010864246,-0.015248873,0.019015038,0.0064176642,-0.0214777,0.02824542,0.0023819127,-0.005676792,0.004700284,0.018489022,0.01210279,0.0065715136,-0.008090901,0.013234929,-0