cmnf5wwcs0011uxs37kcr279e	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	by distributing packages (e.g., npm, pip).\nQ17. What are abstract data types (ADT)? Explain with example.\nAn Abstract Data Type (ADT) is a mathematical model for a data type defined by its behavior\n(operations and their semantics) rather than by its implementation. An ADT specifies what\noperations can be performed on the data, not how they are implemented. The implementation is\nhidden; users interact only with the defined interface.\nThe term was coined by Barbara Liskov and John Guttag. An ADT consists of: a domain (the set of\nvalid values), a set of operations, and axioms (rules governing the operations).\nExample: Stack ADT\nA Stack ADT is defined by these operations: push(item) — adds item to top, pop() — removes and\nreturns top item, peek() — returns top item without removing, isEmpty() — returns true if stack is\nempty, size() — returns number of items.\nThe ADT does not specify whether the stack is implemented using an array or a linked list. Both	[-0.005021683,-1.309846e-05,0.019410511,-0.060579192,-0.022617653,0.0051057497,0.023393963,0.0030788332,0.025479905,-0.0017530663,0.007607279,0.00465177,0.0047349236,0.012664106,0.12815236,0.007998615,-0.009078247,-0.003908036,0.011766383,-0.02021986,0.018623553,-0.008259578,-0.0017201555,-0.021797461,0.010103655,-0.01327746,0.0094658695,-0.0019925458,0.042380817,0.011674408,-0.022257747,0.030213255,0.0039596264,0.016430875,-0.007262921,0.0061903414,-0.011172668,-0.001141798,0.01639454,0.019152634,-0.009612194,0.007304849,-0.0015457951,-0.013119289,-0.008250629,0.012775086,0.0134796025,-0.00842103,-0.04113795,0.0134328585,0.01716775,-0.00822589,0.001506399,-0.18650888,0.015507951,-0.0046871034,-0.011197885,0.0076616835,0.0020218056,-0.0031372847,0.001703458,-0.007476219,-0.012474982,0.015987875,0.0030633768,-0.014664014,0.029627526,-0.0074262344,-0.022051698,-0.00080775295,0.01939837,-0.0030863446,-0.00031189207,-0.008462596,0.013943683,-0.017938236,0.015272