cmnf5wx5z0013uxs32b1acrie	cmnf5wccs0003uxs3mc5evhul	Q18. Explain the concept of scope and visibility in programming languages.\nScope\nScope refers to the region of a program within which a variable or identifier is known and can be\naccessed. It defines the lifetime and accessibility of a name. Scoping rules determine how name\nreferences are resolved in a program.\nStatic (Lexical) Scope: Name references are resolved based on the physical structure of the\nsource code — specifically, where the variable is declared in the code. Most modern languages (C,\nJava, Python) use static scoping. This is determined at compile time.\nDynamic Scope: Name references are resolved based on the calling sequence of functions at\nruntime — the most recently active binding of a name is used. Some older languages (early LISP,\nPerl with local) use dynamic scoping.\nVisibility\nVisibility is related to scope but focuses on whether a particular declaration of a name is accessible	[-0.02692352,-0.001683937,0.008966449,-0.06592101,-0.00405369,0.018400587,-0.0102098165,0.007970585,0.022614954,-0.0064475867,-0.0020643375,-0.001209063,-0.02273416,0.0058224564,0.10832089,-0.001415753,-0.024266277,-0.005232177,0.030396165,-0.028043486,0.014525405,0.017118605,-0.010944611,-0.02771084,-0.0037229494,-0.008265408,0.01244027,0.02563222,0.03456154,0.003399344,-0.015925527,0.031130742,0.020948766,0.02621438,-0.018484639,0.019108525,0.0044047683,-0.008206506,0.0017679995,0.0163686,-0.028233638,-6.438198e-06,0.014413577,-0.00052715145,-0.01257549,0.0021197554,-0.00077956886,-0.026001116,-0.024042225,0.0058992426,-0.0003101336,0.0052581774,-0.015093927,-0.1969971,0.0050195106,-0.00915001,0.00434119,0.015858518,-0.0056570456,0.0027338234,-0.013078668,-0.013010978,-0.021901827,0.0013813403,0.026171263,-0.007150431,0.016172668,0.00093585113,-0.029377656,0.0061923754,0.015128773,0.013658146,0.0026742436,-0.007227607,-0.0060788505,-0.007620957,0.0072448803,-0.05377437,0.0010478953,0.02442677,-0.026951345,-0